Exclude unrelated methods, commands and configuration objects from the primary process parameter inventory.

Retain relevant diagnostic and instrument status nodes in a separate inventory.

## 8. Task D — UNICORN Instrument Initialization Assessment

The UNICORN OPC UA session is established, and the address space is browsable.

The following diagnostic values have been observed:

| Parameter                  | Observed value |
| -------------------------- | -------------- |
| AssignState                | 2 — None       |
| InstrumentStatus           | 2 — Unknown    |
| InstrumentStatusConnection | 0 — Yes        |
| RunState                   | No data        |
| UserInControl              | No data        |
| ConnectSystemTag           | 0 — Good       |

Most instrument state tags currently report Good_NoData.

Consult the UNICORN OPC Server 1.3 User Manual, document 29752660 AA:

- Section 2.5 — OPC UA client connectivity.
- Section 2.7.1, page 29 — ConnectSystem method.
- Section 4.5.1, page 68 — AssignState.
- Section 4.5.4, page 74 — InstrumentStatus.
- Section 4.5.5, page 76 — InstrumentStatusConnection.

Determine whether the OPC client requires an explicit instrument connection before process data becomes available.

Discover and document:

- ConnectSystem parent ObjectId.
- ConnectSystem MethodId.
- Method input and output arguments.
- ConnectSystemTag NodeId.
- ConnectSystemTag data type and access level.
- AssignState NodeId.
- Disconnect method and tag identifiers.
- Available process data nodes.

Verify the supported Ignition 8.3 mechanism for invoking the connection method.

The required instrument assignment is View mode, represented by AssignState = 0.

Do not set AssignState = 1.

Do not assume the ConnectSystemTag trigger value.

Do not execute initialization methods or writes without separate authorization. If initialization is required, produce an exact follow-on execution procedure that establishes View mode without taking control of the instrument.

## 9. Task E — Customer Parameter Mapping

Compare every in-scope customer-requested parameter against the discovered OPC address space.

Evaluate mappings in the following order:

1. Exact NodeId or customer-specified address match.
2. Exact browse path match.
3. Exact parameter name match within the correct instrument.
4. Normalized parameter name match.
5. Engineering unit, description and equipment context match.

Do not treat a fuzzy name match as a verified mapping without supporting evidence.

Classify each parameter as one of the following:

- VERIFIED_LIVE
- VERIFIED_ADDRESS_NO_DATA
- ADDRESS_CORRECTION_REQUIRED
- CANDIDATE_MATCH
- AMBIGUOUS_MATCH
- NOT_EXPOSED
- ACCESS_DENIED
- INITIALIZATION_REQUIRED
- NOT_TESTED

Preserve the original customer parameter naming and addressing alongside the discovered OPC information.

## 10. Task F — Live Data Verification

For all matched, readable parameters:

- Perform an initial read.
- Where practical, establish a temporary read-only subscription.
- Observe the values and timestamps over a bounded monitoring period of approximately 60 to 120 seconds.
- Record initial and final data quality.
- Identify null values, stale values and unsupported data types.
- Determine whether each parameter is available during the current instrument operating state.

Distinguish between:

- Valid data with Good quality.
- Good_NoData.
- Bad quality.
- Uncertain quality.
- Valid but unchanged process values.
- Parameters available only during an active run.
- Parameters dependent on instrument initialization.

Do not classify a static process value as failed solely because it does not change during the observation period.

Do not initiate a process run to generate test data.

## 11. Task G — Existing Project Mapping Comparison

Compare the verified OPC addresses against existing project tag definitions and data acquisition configurations.

Identify:

- Incorrect NodeIds.
- Incorrect OPC connection references.
- Incorrect equipment associations.
- Missing customer parameters.
- Duplicate mappings.
- Incorrect or incompatible data types.
- Engineering unit discrepancies.
- Existing placeholder addresses.
- Parameters mapped to unavailable equipment.

Where available, trace each verified parameter through the existing project configuration to its intended historian measurement or field.

Do not modify the existing configuration during this commission.

Generate a proposed corrected mapping for review.

